<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wolfenbüttel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wolfenbüttel is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oker">Oker</a> south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>. From the 15th century until 1753 it was the residence of the dukes of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel, who built a large palace and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzog-august-library">Herzog August Library</a>, one of the most renowned early modern libraries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthold-ephraim-lessing">Gotthold Ephraim Lessing</a> worked there as librarian from 1770.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel.jpg" alt="Wolfenbüttel" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan-Herm Janßen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wolfenbuettel_Schloss_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Luke found at Arsinoë in Egypt in 1907 and destroyed by water damage in 1945, after it had been copied; four leaves of Romans at <b>Wolfenbüttel</b>; a lead plate from Pannonia with verses of the gospels; a calendar, glosses, and two deeds on papyrus from Naples and Arezzo.</blockquote><a href="/language/gothic">Gothic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>His lands were divided among the Duchy of Brunswick-<b>Wolfenbüttel</b>, the Counts of Schwarzburg and the Counts of Stolberg, and the Klettenberg and Lohra portions later passed to Brandenburg in 1648.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-hohnstein">County of Hohnstein</a></li><li><blockquote>When Otto Cocles died in 1463, the principality passed to the <b>Wolfenbüttel</b> branch and was later joined to the Principality of Calenberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-gottingen">Principality of Göttingen</a></li><li><blockquote>After further partitions the <b>Wolfenbüttel</b> line took shape in the 15th century, with its residence at the castle of Wolfenbüttel, as the city of Brunswick largely governed itself.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-wolfenbuttel">Principality of Wolfenbüttel</a></li></ul></section>
